n after sauron's  nazgul are born  during this era middle earth's noblest  and most powerful men  are the numenoreans who hail from the  island nation of numenor  eventually a vast numenorian army sails  east to aid the elves and put an end to  sauron's rule over middle-earth  faced with inevitable defeat sauron  concocts a new plan  and surrenders willingly to the  numenoreans who take him back to their  homeland as a prisoner  during his time on numenor however  sauron corrupts the king  and persuades him to invade the undying  lands this goes badly  to say the least and numenor itself is  sunk beneath the sea  when numenor sinks sauron goes down with  it losing his physical form for the  first time  but his spirit doesn't die instead it  returns to the mainland  and takes a new shape the silmarillion  explains that  once back in mordor sauron brooded in  the dark until he had wrought for  himself a new shape  and it was terrible for his fair  semblance had departed forever when he  was cast into the abyss at the drowning  of numenor  unfortunately for sauron it turns out  that he wasn't the only survivor of the  numenorean catastrophe  a group of numenoreans known as the  faithful managed to escape in their  ships having decided not to join the  invasion of valinor  arriving in middle-earth the faithful  set up two sister kingdoms  arnor in the north and gondor in the  south  seeking justice against sauron the  exiled numenoreans joined forces with  the elves and dwarves of middle earth  sparking the conflict known as the war  of the last alliance  the free armies win a number of  successes against sauron and eventually  lay siege to mordor itself  during the final battle isildur prince  of the faithful  cuts the one ring from sauron's hand and  with this  the second age of middle-earth comes to  an end and the dark lord's shapeless  spirit takes flight once more  sauron is absent from the history books  of the early third age  the appendix to the return of the king  succinctly explains that during this  time  sauron slept and the one ring was lost  when he next appears sauron is known  only as the necromancer  a name that has a great deal of  significance to tolkien's writings  by this point sauron has taken on a  number of shapes forms and names  the necromancer persona however is  distantly connected to the earliest  version of tolkien's arch villain  in the earliest versions of the stories  that would later appear in the  silmarillion  one of morgoth's chief lieutenants is  tevildau the so-called prince of cats  over time as tolkien characteristically  rewrote his narrative many times  the character was eventually replaced by  a being named thu  who is described as a magician a  sorcerer and a werewolf  he's also the earliest clearly  identifiable version of sauron in  tolkien's mythos  interestingly it's this early  underdeveloped and eventually abandoned  iteration of sauron that is the most  closely aligned with his later  appearance as the necromancer  in fact tolkien's posthumously published  book the lays of belarian  even refers to thu this way explaining  in glamory that necromancer held his  hosts of phantoms and of wandering  ghosts  so why does the power hungry dark lord  ultimately become associated with the  title of the necromancer at this point  in his story  the answer seems to lie in tolkien's  simple desire to connect his first  wildly successful book the hobbit to his  larger  than private mythology according to  tolkien himself  the necromancer was a necessary peril  who added depth to bilbo baggins world  in a letter to his publisher tolkien  explained that the presence of the  terrible  is i believe what gives this imagined  world its very similitude  a safe fairyland is untrue to all worlds  initially the necromancer was meant only  as a veiled reference to sauron himself  who was unknown to the public at the  time but his identity revealed itself as  the hobbit nudged a little closer to the  world of tolkien's other works  in another letter tolkien writes mr  baggins began  as a comic tale among conventional and  inconsistent grimm's fairy tale dwarfs  and got drawn into the edge of the  legendarium so that even sauron the  terrible peeped over the edge  but the hobbit was never supposed to  have been consistently integrated  with the stories of the silmarillion  meaning tolkien appears to have thrown  the character in  as a half thought out tip of the hat for  his own personal amusement  then he wrote the lord of the rings  however and everything changed  with sauron in the one ring taking  center stage for the sequel  it suddenly became important to  reconcile the necromancer of mirkwood  with morgoth second in command  sauron in the appendix to the return of  the king  sauron's re-emergence is first mentioned  roughly 1050 years into the third age  at which time a shadow falls on  greenwood and men begin to call it  merkwood  this is not the greenwood although  the very air of the forest is heavy with  illusion  over the next 2000 years sauron slowly  builds his power  and attempts to take a new form  tolkien's details are very scarce during  this time  and there's some contention regarding  whether he succeeds in taking on a  physical shape at this point  while it's possible that the necromancer  is spiritual in nature however  it's worth pointing out that sauron is  mentioned as having a physical body in  the novel version of the lord of the  rings  so he seems to have taken shape  eventually still  physical former no the necromancer has  little trouble sewing chaos in middle  earth from behind the scenes  fifty years after sauron's shadow was  first felt in merkwood  the wizards arrive in middle earth with  the express purpose of resisting the  resurgent darkness  over the next few centuries several  events begin to unfold  setting into motion sauron's final  return in southern merkwood the elvish  city of aman lok  becomes dogudur the hill of sorcery  and it's assumed by many that a  ringwraith or some other nameless evil  has taken up residence there  meanwhile easterlings begin to push  westward displacing local settlers  these corrupted men used to serve sauron  and their arrival gives their old master  some local muscle to man his new  fortress  this also seems to be what causes the  hobbits who live just west of merkwood  to migrate further west over the misty  mountains and settle in the shire  by the 1300th year of the age the will  have officially returned and  evil is beginning to stir pretty much  everywhere  then roughly 1 600 years into the third  age  a sickness called the great plague  devastates middle earth  in the return of the king tolkien writes  that the plague came with dark winds out  of the east  and also points out that later it was  noted that these things happened  even as the shadow grew deep in mirkwood  and many evil things reappeared  signs of the arising of sauron about a  thousand years after sauron relocates to  dolgodur  several of his enemies begin to suspect  that the dark lord himself is hiding in  murkwood  rather than a lone ringwraith as a  result gandalf the gray decides to go  and investigate  the necromancer however isn't ready to  reveal himself  when gandalf arrives in dolgodur sauron  opts to flee for the time being  so that the grey wizard doesn't discover  who he really is  the dark lord withdraws far into the  eastern regions of middle earth  in the meantime the western portion of  the continent enjoys a 400 year stretch  of peace and quiet known as the watchful  peace  during this time sauron continues to  prepare from afar  and his servants in earth remain  relatively quiet  at the end of the watchful peace sauron  returns to dougaldore  with greater power and more servants in  tow coincidentally just three short  years later  gollum happens to stumble on the one  ring and the white council is officially  formed to fight the growing shadow in  middle earth  for almost 500 years both the white  council and the necromancer by their  time  eventually gandalf returns to dog guldur  in search of answers  this time he discovers that the  necromancer really is sauron  that the dark lord is gathering together  the rings of power  and that he's desperately searching for  the one ring gandalf also learns that  the enemy is trying to locate and hunt  down the heirs of his silder  doubt evernote the room has reached  the heir of newman still lives gandalf  also runs into thorin's father  thrain at this point and the captured  dwarven king gives him a map of erebor  as well as the key that bilbo later uses  to unlock the secret door to the lonely  mountain  equipped with this new knowledge gandolf  urges the white council to attack the  necromancer stronghold  saruman the white however delays the  assault insisting that the loss of the  one ring means that sauron isn't a real  threat to middle earth  saruman isn't actively aiding sauron at  this point but the white wizard wants to  observe the dark lord's movements  in the hopes that he'll find out where  sauron thinks the one ring is  so he can find and claim it for himself  zaruman's scheming sets back the white  council's attack by several decades  until in the same year that the events  of the hobbit take place  they finally attacked the necromancer in  dulgaldur  the fighting isn't described in any of  tolkien's works and it's unsure whether  the council took on sauron alone  or whether the attack was more of a  full-scale battle  details aside the necromancer appears to  be defeated  and retreats only to later reappear in  mordor  safely ensconced in his ancient home he  finally  openly declares himself as none other  than the dark lord sauron  soon after the war of the rings begins  frodo baggins comes into possession of  the one ring  and sauron the deceiver is defeated once  and for all  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus  even more looper videos about your  